course: dictionary
lesson: 7753
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives several, so you must use the context to choose the right one. «We spent the afternoon on the river, going punting with a long pole.» The words river and pole tell you that a punt here is a boat.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «Dan dropped the ball and punted it down the field.» Ball and field tell you that punt is a kick in a sport, not a boat.
explain: Informal words change meaning again. «I'll take a punt on that horse» has no boat and no ball. Take a punt means take a risk or make a guess. Always ask: which meaning fits the words around it?
fact: Punt can be a noun (a punt, a flat boat) or a verb (to punt, to travel by pushing a pole).
fact: A punt is a long, flat-bottomed boat with square ends, pushed along a river with a long pole.
fact: In sport, a punt is a kick: the ball is dropped from the hands and kicked before it touches the ground.
fact: Informally, to take a punt means to have a go or make a guess, as in «take a punt on it».
fact: In Irish history, the punt was Ireland's money before the euro came in 2002.
exercise: «We spent the afternoon punting along the river.» What is a punt in this sentence? | A school bag with wheels | *A flat boat pushed along with a long pole | A bird that swims in ponds | A type of hat worn in the rain
exercise: Ollie says, «I'll take a punt on that horse.» What does take a punt mean? | Walk the horse slowly | Pay for a ticket to the stables | *Have a go, a risky bet or a guess | Lose a race on purpose
exercise: «Dan dropped the ball and punted it down the field.» What does punted mean here? | *Kicked the ball just after dropping it | Caught the ball in both hands | Threw the ball over the fence | Pushed a boat with a pole
test: «Toad's crazes so far: punting, then house-boating.» What is punting? | Reading a map of the sea | Riding a bicycle along the shore | *Pushing a flat boat along a river with a long pole | Swimming across a lake
test: «In Ireland, the punt was the money before the euro.» What was the punt? | A kind of sports ball | A small flat boat | *Ireland's money before the euro in 2002 | A long pole for the river
exam: Mia says, «Let's take a punt and see what happens.» Which meaning fits best? | Pay for it in Irish money | Kick a ball as far as you can | *Have a go and see, taking a guess | Get into a boat and row across the lake
